WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.100
How can well-meaning, data-driven,
attempts to solve social problems

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.520
actually make things worse?
Campbell's Law may hold the answer.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.100
Crafted by a social scientist nearly
a half-century ago,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:23.220
the law basically says: "when a measure
becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure."

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.380
The problems play out in many
fields, including education.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:32.380
Attempts to evaluate schools and teachers based on
reading and math scores cause educators

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.480
to do everything possible to boost
results – whether that's narrowing curricula,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.800
going goofy for test
preparation, or cooking the books.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.620
It brings to mind those old Soviet
factories, with their production quotas

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.740
and shoddy products. But Campbell's Law
applies to more than test scores.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.300
We've seen it play out when schools are told to
improve school safety numbers or

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.180
to drive up graduation rates. Now, none of
this means that we should shy away from

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:00.359
accountability or measurement in
education. But it does mean that we need

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:05.840
to be a lot more attentive to how those
metrics get used. They need to respect the law.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.900
Campbell's law, that is.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.380
How do
you think we should tackle

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.780
accountability in American education?
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:20.500
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:28.460
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

